[human #4] now we're going to work on the actual audit contents. we're going to remove the entire preamble: ``` Historical automated evidence for authored examples, not a verdict on this component or verification of the current code. Settings: WCAG 2.2 AA. Library accessibility option: n/a. Supported schemes: rebrand light/dark, Canvas light, and Canvas High Contrast light. Unsupported historical Canvas dark cases are excluded. The original run didn't record an exact source revision. Later fixes aren't included unless the evidence is refreshed. Dates, tool versions, original run IDs, and results-file SHA-256 digests are retained in the downloadable evidence. ```
[human #5] we're also going to remove: * the download audit results link * the following prose: ``` Page and npm evidence aren't equivalent. Missing tiers, undetermined checks, and not-applicable results aren't passes. Supplemental npm states may lack interaction, computed, and condition checks. Simulated screen-reader output isn't real assistive technology. Automated checks can't judge meaningful labels, reading order in real use, all visual overlap, or JavaScript-driven motion. ``` ### Evidence details and review items the entire section. keep: ``` These automated results do not establish that the page meets WCAG; manual review is still needed. ```
[human #6] now for the tables we can drop the IBM content, and then instead of focusing on axe-core, we're going to focus on WCAG criteria that was tested so it should look like multiple tables or tables with multiple heading/section rows: ``` checks | Rebrand (light) | Rebrand (dark) | Canvas | Canvas High Contrast --- conditions --- (condition name / WCAG criteria) | pass | pass | fail | pass --- interactions --- ... --- computed --- ... screen reader --- ... ``` and there should be pass | fail | indeterminate (or a better label) and we should be using pantoken instui-pills
[human #7] in the layout the lets do in the conditions column: ``` condition-name WCAG 2.2 N.N.N ``` and then set word-wrap allowed on the condition name. the pills should not word wrap.
[human #8] now tags should be: * Pass * Fail * Not applicable and some sort of "needs review" or "manual testing required" or "unknown" or something that lets the user know they need to validate. and each pill should have an accompanying icon.
[human #9] if an entire row is "Not applicable" for a component then just don't output that row.
[human #10] is axe-core only testing 8 things in automatica11y? the Conditions table only has 8 rows.
[human #11] yeah, we should be blending the axe results with the automatica11y results into wcag-aligned results tables. we can keep IBM out because it doesn't align to WCAG.
[human #12] when there's a failure, or a Mnaual review required then the label for that row should stretch across two rows, and there should be a second row that spans all the brands that includes the details. for example Alert's forced-colors-focus-visible fails across all themes.
[human #13] change the "Manual review needed" to "Review needed"
[human #14] for avatar the results table says color-contrast needs manual review, my hunch is because either: we don't have an `-automatica11y` flag on the cssdoc OR it is testing this example but the -color-ai variant is causing issues b/c of its gradient. if it's the latter we should be able to include that in the details of the needs review section.
[human #15] we don't need to assume the reason, but it should be clear to the user that it's the -color-ai variant that needs review if the others don't have issues. This should be true of ALL of them.
[human #16] the affected should be a list and it should be properly backticked or a code block.
[human #17] now, we should be able to derive from the code that throws the needs review which part or modifier or area of the cssdoc / example caused it instead of passing all the content back.
[human #18] > Doing that is possible but would tie the importer to the component records. Say if you want it. what does this mean?
[human #19] agent-shell says accessibility not audited. all components should be audited.
[human #20] layout plugin doesn't need to be covered. for both components and custom-components go through the utilities, declarations, etc. and add those audits where applicable.
[human #21] now, the accessibility audit and output should be part of the docs build process so that it just works and is always current when we do releases and updates. that also means that we should be cacheing and only updating when there have been changes for a component. It should also easily feed into the translation pipeline so all strings in the accessibility section and generated tables should be able to be pushed through the translation pipeline.
[assistant] I'll start by looking at how the docs build, the translation pipeline and the audit currently fit together.
